  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1108 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nDry and mild conditions will prevail through late week, under an\nunseasonably strong upper level ridge. This ridge will extend\nfrom east TX to the lower Great Lakes by Tuesday evening, while\nthe remnants of a southern branch trough remain along central\nGulf coast. The upper ridge will further amplify over the OH\nValley and Mid-Atlantic region by late Wednesday. This will\nbriefly place the lower MS River Valley under sufficient\nsouthwest flow aloft to lift remnant energy from the Gulf coast\ninto AL. At this time, northeast MS appears to miss out on any\nlight rain showers Wednesday night and early Thursday.\n\nUpper level ridging will amplify from TX into the lower OH River\nValley Thursday night into early Friday. The latest medium range\nmodel consensus has trended slightly more amplified in the\ndepiction of a progressive northern branch trough lifting through\nthe central plains on Saturday. Relative to yesterday's\nsolutions, slightly stronger southwesterly flow is depicted by\nLREF over the Midsouth, coincident with PWAT around 1 inch and\nsurface dewpoints around 60F. Deterministic solutions from the\nGFS and ECMWF depict light QPF over the Midsouth Saturday, but\nLREF mean QPF and NBM are considerably lower, perhaps partly due\nto meager convective instability. The takeaway: light showers\ncannot be ruled out on Saturday, but impacts should be minimal. \nSunday may hold better rain chances given more consequential \nmidlevel height falls and associated cold frontal passage.\n\nShortwave ridging aloft will transit the Midsouth on Monday.\nThereafter, model consensus begins to fade, with the GFS ensemble\nsolutions favoring a high amplitude progressive trough ejecting\nfrom the southern Rockies into the Great Plains and MS River\nValley toward the middle of next week. ECMWF deterministic\nmirrors the EC consensus in maintaining less amplified flow and\nless eventful weather over the central CONUS in the days prior to\nthe Thanksgiving holiday.",
